"A role-permission matrix compares defined permissions across roles while keeping assignment and inheritance rules explicit.\n\nUse when: Review least-privilege role design across many capabilities. Spot broad grants or separation-of-duties conflicts.\n\nAvoid when: Conditional policies and indirect privilege paths dominate access decisions.\n\nVisual grammar: Rows identify roles and columns identify actions on resource classes. Cells distinguish allowed, denied, inherited, and unspecified status under a legend. Separate annotations state user assignment and role-hierarchy rules.\n\nBuild: Choose a bounded resource and action vocabulary. Populate cells from policy evidence or mark the design proposed. Separate inherited grants from direct grants when they affect review. Use clear column headers, padded cells, and minimal rules that never cross text.\n\nCheck: Unspecified does not silently mean explicit deny. Permissions describe actions and resource scope precisely. Role hierarchy and conflicting assignments are evaluated separately. Status remains readable without relying solely on color.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a role name as evidence of its actual permissions. Omitting resource scope and making narrow and broad grants appear equivalent.\n\nExample: Support may read Orders, Billing may refund Payments, and only an approved administrative role may change access policy.\n\nAlternatives: Authorization graph, Zero-trust policy diagram."
